ts 25th in EPA per play. Their most distinctive trait is the lowest under center rate in the league.","offense":"The Browns offense has struggled, ranking 25th or worse in three of the five main efficiency measures. Their run and pass mix has stayed close to what game situations call for. Their biggest outliers in how they line up are the lowest under center rate and the highest shotgun rate in the league. Deshaun Watson has run the passing game, with Denzel Boston leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Browns defense has been a little above average by EPA allowed per play so far. It has been much better against the pass than the run, ranking 10th and 27th. Maliek Collins has been as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise is Mason Graham, who has 4 sacks despite playing only 58% of the snaps. Their scheme has been fairly conventional, and the closest thing to a signature is a number of defenders in the box that ranks 6th lowest in the league.","special":"The Browns have been about average in how often they go for it on fourth down. Their kickers have made 89% of field goals, which is a little above average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into the quarterback, about 21% of the money on the books. Their receivers were among the youngest in the league.","players":"Deshaun Watson has played nearly every snap at quarterback and thrown for 1,806 yards. Targets have been spread around, with Denzel Boston and Harold Fannin Jr. leading a group where nobody has more than a fifth of the looks. Quinshon Judkins has handled most of the work in the backfield with 69% of the carries. On defense, Mason Graham leads the team with 4 sacks and Denzel Ward has picked off 3 passes.","coaching":"Todd Monken is calling the offense as head coach and Mike Rutenberg is calling the defense. Their offense looks most like the 2023 and 2022 Jaguars. Their defense looks most like the 2025 Seahawks under Mike Macdonald and the 2023 Saints under Dennis Allen.","trends":"The offense went from 31st in EPA per play in 2025 to 25th so far this season, a modest improvement. The defense moved from 5th in EPA allowed per play in 2025 to 14th so far this season, a big step back. The biggest change in approach was number of defenders in the box, which went from 6.42 to 6.29.","notes":"The 2026 pages are built from data that nflverse updates every week, so they will keep changing until the season ends. Personnel, formations, coverage and pressure numbers are missing for now because that data is only released after the season."}}